course: dictionary
lesson: 11381
title: Abscise: when plants drop their parts
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: «In autumn, the oak tree begins to abscise its leaves.» Abscise is a hard word, but the clues are autumn and leaves. Leaves fall from trees in autumn, so abscise must mean to drop.
explain: Plants do this on purpose. A special layer of cells forms where the leaf joins the stem, and then the leaf falls off. Scientists call this layer the abscission layer.
explain: In everyday English, we say drop or shed. «The plum tree will shed its blossom in May» means the same as «The plum tree will abscise its blossom in May». Check a new word by swapping in an everyday word and seeing if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Abscise is a verb: abscise, abscises, abscised, abscising.
fact: «Abscise» means that a plant drops a leaf, flower or fruit by forming a special layer of cells where it joins the stem.
fact: The special layer of cells is called the abscission layer.
fact: «Abscise» can also mean to cut something off. This meaning is more formal.
fact: It is a rare, technical word. In everyday English, say drop or shed.
exercise: «In autumn, the oak tree begins to abscise its leaves.» What does abscise mean here? | *To drop | To sing | To paint | To water
exercise: Which everyday word could replace abscise in «The plant will abscise its old flowers»? | climb | whistle | *shed | sleep
exercise: What is the special layer of cells called, where a leaf joins the stem? | leaf shade | root layer | stem paint | *abscission layer
test: «The gardener watched the plum tree abscise its blossom in May.» What does abscise mean in this sentence? | To paint the blossom white | To water the roots | *To drop the blossom from the tree | To plant new blossom on the tree
test: Apart from dropping leaves, flowers or fruit, what else can abscise mean in formal English? | To grow taller | To freeze solid | *To cut something off | To whisper quietly
exam: Which sentence uses abscise correctly? | The river will abscise by tomorrow | *Trees abscise their leaves in autumn | I abscised my breakfast quickly | Birds abscise their songs at night
